Although many people doubted that a nation deeply committed to Marxism-Leninism-Maoism could reinvent itself into a thriving market economy, as early as 1980 some astute observers noticed the sincerity of the commitments Chinese leadership had made to economic liberalization beginning in the late 1970s. One such observer was Milton Friedman. Quoting a report he wrote in 1980 on Deng Xiaoping's efforts to unshackle the Chinese economy from paralyzing top-down control, Friedman remarked years later that he had viewed Chinese economic liberalization as "one of the great economic experiments of our time". In that same report, he elaborated further: "The reforms – the attempt to introduce market elements, the opening up of contacts with the West, the encouragement of foreign investment, and so on – are in a desirable direction....The potential is enormous. The present level is extremely low....My own conjecture is that there will be considerable progress in the next few years as some of the newly announced policies work themselves through the system."[^2] The Singaporean leader Lee Kuan Yew was even more optimistic, saying in 1980 "\[INSERT LEE KUAN YEW'S OBSERVATIONS\]"\[\^3\]

