China's economy and China's stocks, each measured against America's. Every quantity is China's divided by America's, so the United States is the numéraire, and every change is in bits. 1 bit = ln 2 ≈ 0.693 nats: one doubling. Top: real GDP per capita, up 4.02 bits (×16) since 1978. That is the change, not the level: in 2025 China's GDP per capita was still ×0.20 of America's, 2.30 bits (about 2.3 doublings) short, in constant 2015 US\$ at market exchange rates; purchasing-power parity would show a smaller gap. Bottom: MSCI China ÷ MSCI USA (periwinkle) against the same economy line re-based to December 1998 (lime), shaded periwinkle where the stocks outpace the economy and grey where they lag it. The stock ratio fell to −0.78 bits (×0.58) in January 2002, peaked at +1.66 bits (×3.2) in October 2007, and touched its low of −0.84 bits (×0.56) in August 2024; by June 2025, the last month both datasets cover, the economy ratio was up 2.23 bits (×4.7) and the stock ratio down 0.57 bits (×0.67). Later stock data are not shown. Sources: World Bank, World Development Indicators (CC BY 4.0), GDP per capita in constant 2015 US\$; MSCI, MSCI China and MSCI USA net total return indexes in US dollars.
